# PX4 parameters for flying a TERRAIN-FOLLOWING plan whose legs carry large
# altitude steps — so the mission sequencer does not stall on a waypoint the
# vehicle cannot hit to centimetre vertical accuracy.
# Format: <vehicle_id> <component_id> <name> <value> <type>  (type 6 = int32, 9 = float)
#
# Apply ON TOP OF sitl_bringup.params (and the GPS-denied set if flying that way):
#   droneflight px4-params --file configs/px4/sitl_bringup.params \
#                          --file configs/px4/terrain_following.params
# then paste the `param set` lines into pxh and `reboot`.
#
# WHY THIS EXISTS
# The planner's CFIT fix (droneplanner SHORTFALLS item 0) splits a descending leg
# whose flown profile would hit terrain, inserting one vertex so the autopilot
# cannot get ahead of the ground. That works — flown 2026-07-27
# (`sim-logs/cfit/01_36_02.ulg`), minimum AGL went from 0.8 m (the 2026-07-24
# crash on this exact leg) to 11.97 m, with zero samples under 5 m.
#
# But PX4 will not SEQUENCE PAST a mission waypoint until the vehicle is inside
# BOTH the horizontal acceptance radius (NAV_ACC_RAD, 10 m) and the vertical one
# (NAV_MC_ALT_RAD, 0.8 m by default). An inserted vertex sits mid-descent by
# construction, so the vehicle passes it while still coming down:
#
#   run              inserted vertex   best |alt error| inside NAV_ACC_RAD
#   2026-07-27 v1    leg 10 (unneeded) 1.58 m   -> froze at 11/79
#   2026-07-27 v2    leg 50 (needed)   0.87 m   -> froze at 51/78, by 7 cm
#
# A stall is silent and does not look like a crash: `mission progress` simply
# stops advancing while the vehicle keeps flying the last trajectory. The v1 run
# slid past its frozen waypoint for 565 s, descending, until it touched down.
#
# WHY WIDENING THE WINDOW IS THE RIGHT LEVER, NOT A PLANNER CHANGE
# No insertion placement can avoid this. PX4 spreads a leg's descent across the
# leg (measured gradient 0.040 for an 18 m step, 0.220 for a 40 m one), so the
# vehicle is descending at EVERY interior point of a descending leg — there is no
# "settled" position to insert at. The altitude the plan asks for is still flown;
# only the tolerance for declaring the waypoint reached changes.
#
# It costs nothing on ordinary waypoints. Measured over 51 accepted arrivals
# (2026-07-24) PX4 held altitude to 0.35 m mean / 0.64 m p95, and every original
# waypoint on the 2026-07-27 route tracked to <=0.37 m — all far inside even the
# default window, so widening it changes their behaviour not at all. It only
# affects waypoints the vehicle genuinely cannot hit tightly.
#
# MEASURED OVER 12 FLIGHTS (2026-07-27, `sim-logs/cfit/` + `cfit-rep/` + `cfit-rep2/`).
# Outcome is decided by ONE rule, with no exceptions in 12 runs: the mission
# stalls iff the vertical error at some waypoint exceeds this window.
#
#   window   err at inserted vertex   err at final wp   runs   outcome
#   0.8      2.147 m                  -                 2      0/2 COMPLETE
#   2.0      0.664 / 0.725 / 2.569 m  1.75-1.80 m       5      4/5 COMPLETE
#   4.0      0.669-0.719 m            1.78-1.83 m       5      5/5 COMPLETE
#
# Two distinct populations, and both matter:
#   * The INSERTED vertex is bimodal - usually 0.66-0.73 m, but it spikes to
#     2.1-2.6 m in roughly 1 run in 6. Those spikes are the stalls.
#   * The FINAL waypoint sits at ~1.78 m in every healthy run. A 0.8 m window
#     would fail there too; the 0.8 runs simply never got that far.
#
# 4.0 covers the entire observed distribution (worst 2.569 m) with 1.56x margin.
# ⚠️ The 5/5 at 4.0 does NOT independently prove it - none of those five saw a
# spike (max 1.83 m), so they would have passed at 2.0 as well. The evidence for
# 4.0 is the error DISTRIBUTION across all 12 runs, not that run of five.
#
# ⚠️ This is empirical tuning against a quantity we cannot measure cleanly. PX4
# tests its *own estimate*, and the error figures above are groundtruth — a proxy.
# It predicts all seven outcomes correctly, which is why it is trusted here, but a
# `vehicle_local_position`-based calculation does NOT work (its z is relative to
# the EKF local origin, not the mission home datum, and `ref_alt` is NaN in these
# logs). That mistake produced a confidently wrong "the param is irrelevant"
# conclusion on 2026-07-27.
#
# ⚠️ A value set at the `pxh>` prompt lands in the ULog's `changed_parameters`,
# NOT `initial_parameters`. Check both when auditing a run, or two
# differently-configured flights will look identical — which is what happened.
#
# ALTERNATIVE, if the stall persists: drop the inserted vertex entirely and raise
# the failing leg's END altitude by 14 m instead (measured: that clears the same
# leg to 5.4 m without any new waypoint). It removes the sequencing risk outright
# but costs concealment — 30 m -> 44 m AGL over that stretch — so it is a
# survivability trade, not a free fix. See droneplanner SHORTFALLS item 0.
#
# ⚠️ Like NAV_DLL_ACT and the RTL altitude, this is a deliberate design choice
# and belongs in the aircraft's failsafe/nav policy, not left at a default that
# was tuned for precise survey flights over flat ground.
1	1	NAV_MC_ALT_RAD	4.0	9
